G.append({'heading': 'Corporate actions', 'blocks': [
 p(' The wave, on the filing lines: Oil India\'s standalone profit rose 116% year-on-year to Rs 2,259.42 crore - down 21% on the June quarter - on crude production up 20% and realisations of $92.24 a barrel against $68.19; an interim dividend of Rs 7 a share goes with it, record date 20 November. Vodafone Idea: revenue Rs 11,884 crore, up 2% on the quarter; net loss Rs 5,975 crore, from Rs 3,747 crore; ARPU Rs 180, from Rs 195; margin 41.9%, from 43.1%. HAL: profit Rs 1,839 crore, up 11%; revenue Rs 7,613 crore, up 15%; margin 22.8% against about 25% expected. Bharat Dynamics: revenue Rs 1,644 crore, up 43.3%; profit Rs 183.5 crore, down 15%. IRCTC: profit Rs 263.6 crore, down 23%, on Rail Neer\'s Rs 106.12-crore swing to loss; catering up 26%, tourism up 28%. Hitachi Energy India: revenue Rs 2,996.5 crore, up 64%; profit Rs 319 crore; exports up 41%. Alkem: revenue Rs 4,552 crore, up 14%; profit Rs 667.1 crore, down 14% on the year, up 28% on the quarter. Ola Electric: revenue Rs 421 crore, down 39%; loss Rs 362 crore, from Rs 418 crore; 255 dealer partners and 319 stores, heading for about 450 stores by December and 700 by March 2027 (' + a(CNBC18, 'CNBC-TV18') + '). ONGC\'s Rs 8 interim went ex on Thursday - the price now trades without the dividend. Bosch\'s 3:1 bonus plus 1:5 split awaits the postal ballot (carried). Today brings about 200 more prints: Tata Motors PV, Eicher Motors, Grasim, ABB India, RVNL and Zetwerk among them; Natco Pharma\'s dividend call (carried).'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Finance Goldmine', 'blocks': [
 p(' Seventeen sessions, and the tide kept paying after the ceiling broke. The provisional print: foreigners bought Rs 7,146.17 crore on Thursday - a seventeenth straight buying session on this paper\'s count - while domestic institutions sold Rs 5,775.09 crore (' + a(FEFLOW, 'Financial Express') + '). Now read Thursday\'s two numbers against each other, because they are the same trade seen from opposite ends. The foreign buyer is paying nine-month-high prices for Indian equities while Indian inflation prints 6.09% - which means the tide is betting the RBI\'s December answer is a rate path the rest of the world\'s money can live behind: higher rates defend the currency, and a defended 94-rupee currency protects the foreigner\'s dollar return. The machinery of the print itself, from ' + a(MOSPI, 'the release') + ': a 2024-base index, food and beverages carrying nearly 40% of the basket, rural inflation at 6.63% against urban 5.41% - the countryside, where food weighs heaviest in the household budget, is paying the most. And the arithmetic that frames December: with the repo rate at 5.50% and inflation at 6.09%, India\'s real policy rate is negative again - the gap the poll\'s expected rise to 5.75% would only half close. The rupee closed at 94.04, down 33 paise (' + a(HBL, 'HBL') + '): for now the flow still outruns the fear. What turns this story is whether the tide reads 6.09% as a reason India will hike - or as a reason to leave before it does.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Startup Intelligence', 'blocks': [
 p(' The compliance moat, priced at Rs 32 crore. Desible.ai, a Bengaluru startup founded in 2025 by Uttam Tiwari and Omkar Raikar, raised Rs 32 crore led by Prime Venture Partners, with existing investor Invention Engine participating (' + a(ETDES, 'ET') + '). The product is workflow orchestration for banks, non-bank lenders and insurers - customer engagement, servicing, collections, compliance - begun as voice automation and now spreading to WhatsApp, SMS and email; the platform already runs over 25 workflows, handles more than 10 million customer engagements a month, and is in talks with over 40 BFSI institutions. The founders\' thesis is the sentence the whole agentic wave will be judged by: "In regulated industries, AI that is right most of the time is not enough." So Desible runs what it calls deterministic decisioning - disclosures, consent and escalations executed exactly as defined, every step auditable, aligned to IRDAI, RBI, TRAI and the data-protection act. Prime\'s Gaurav Ranjan is buying the same wedge: AI as "an operational layer for insurers," not a chatbot. The insight worth keeping: in regulated markets the winning agent is not the cleverest one - it is the one a compliance officer can defend in writing, and that constraint, not model quality, is what an incumbent bank will pay for.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'AI Race', 'blocks': [
 p(' Google shipped the fleet; a court was asked to sink it. On Thursday Google introduced Gemini 4 Hydrogen - the fast, cheap execution model to sit under Carbon and Argon, priced flat at $1.50 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output across a one-million-token context, beating Argon on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index at default settings and running 2.5 times faster than the Flash model it replaces - and, with it, Orchestrator inside Antigravity, which switches between Carbon and Hydrogen inside one session and reached near-Carbon success rates using 24% less quota and finishing 19% faster (' + a(GOGH, 'Google\'s own post') + '). The frontier\'s shape is now explicit: one model thinks, another works, a third checks. On the same day, a coalition of online-safety and consumer groups sued Anthropic, Google, Meta, OpenAI and SpaceXSI in San Francisco Superior Court, asking a judge to halt further frontier development entirely - models the filing says their own makers cannot prevent from causing "catastrophic" harm (' + a(APLAW, 'AP, which first reported the filing') + '). The suit lands days after the midterms returned a Congress promising AI regulation - and hours before the industry\'s anchor company lost its founder. The race\'s three forces - capability, law and governance - have never all moved on the same day before. Thursday they did.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Vibe coding', 'blocks': [
 p(' Her embroidery machine wanted a subscription; she wrote it an operating system instead. The builder\'s story on Hacker News this morning (282 points at this writing): a $300 embroidery machine whose maker locks designs behind a subscription - so Kathy Korevec, a product manager who has worked at Google, Vercel, GitHub and Heroku, built Zigzag instead: free, open-source embroidery software for Mac and Windows that turns SVG artwork into machine stitches, edits them visually, and - the part that makes it this week\'s story - lets AI agents design for the machine through MCP, the model-context protocol (' + a(ZIGZAG, 'the repository') + '; ' + a(HNFRONT, 'HN front page') + '). Two builder lessons in one repo. First, the subscription era\'s answer is increasingly personal: when the tool rents itself back to you, a weekend and an agent can repossess it. Second, MCP is quietly becoming the universal socket - the same protocol that lets an agent query a database here lets it thread a needle, literally. The machine cost $300. The software now costs nothing, and it outdesigns the paywall.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Try this today (Friday)', 'blocks': [
 p(' Three reads, in order. First, the primary source behind today\'s loudest number: the statistics office\'s own October CPI release - eleven pages, free, no paywall between you and the 6.09% (' + a(MOSPI, 'the PDF') + '). Read the rural-urban split (6.63% against 5.41%) and ask who the headline average is hiding. Second, at 9:15, watch one level: the street\'s published support is 25,000 (' + a(MCSETUP, 'moneycontrol') + '). A 6.09% print plus $95.82 oil is the first real stress the three-day rally has faced; whether the level holds tells you more than any commentary by 9:30. Third, ten minutes with ' + a(NVREL, 'Nvidia\'s own release') + ' - the whole statement is eight paragraphs - and then Kress\'s staff note as reported by ' + a(AXIOS, 'Axios') + '. Notice what the company chose to say (operations, roadmap, 17 November) and what it chose not to (a reason). In corporate statements, the silence is a disclosure too.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'One concept: key person risk', 'blocks': [
 p(' How much of a company is one human being? Key person risk is the discount a business carries for the answer - the value that walks out of the building if one named person does. Nvidia\'s own proxy statement spells it out in boilerplate that read Thursday like prophecy: the company is "highly dependent on the services of our longstanding executive team," and weak succession planning could "hinder our strategic planning, execution, and long-term success" (' + a(WSJ, 'the Journal notes') + '). Huang ran the place with roughly sixty direct reports and a stated disbelief in succession planning - structure that maximises speed and concentrates risk in the same move. The market has priced this before: Apple traded through the question twice with Steve Jobs; Berkshire Hathaway has carried a Buffett discount for thirty years; Tesla\'s multiple is inseparable from one person\'s attention span. The defenses are dull and visible: a named interim (Kress, Thursday), a board that can act (its first-ever chair, Thursday), a bench the market already knows. Watch how the discount behaves: it opens on the announcement - Thursday\'s double-digit morning - and it closes, or doesn\'t, as the successor question resolves. The practical use for any portfolio, including a portfolio of one\'s own career: wherever value depends on one person continuing to show up, something is mispriced - either the person is underpaid, or everyone else is uninsured.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Term of the day: ex-dividend', 'blocks': [
 p(' The ex-dividend date is the first day a stock trades without its declared dividend - buy on or after it and the seller, not you, collects the payout. ONGC wore the mechanics on Thursday: its Rs 8 interim had 12 November as the record date, so Thursday was the ex date (' + a(INDIAINX, 'the corporate-action circular') + '), and the stock closed at Rs 224.75, down Rs 10.25. About eight of those rupees are not a verdict on the company - they are the dividend leaving the price, the way a house price drops when the furniture is sold separately. The exchange machinery does this deliberately: the buyer on the ex date no longer receives the Rs 8, so the price adjusts by roughly the payout. This is why the setup board reads ONGC\'s fall as mostly arithmetic - the crude leg and the coil floor still decide the line, not the ex-date gap. The checks that matter around any ex date: the announcement date (when the dividend is declared), the record date (who the company pays), the ex date (when the price adjusts) and the payment date (when money moves). Trap for the unwary: a stock "down" on its ex date has often fallen by exactly the dividend - and a yield calculated off the pre-gap price flatters the future. Read prices around dividends the way accountants read them: the stock plus the cash, never the stock alone.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'The setup board (research, not advice)', 'blocks': [
 p(' Thursday scored the held-breath session, and the board\'s oldest line rewrote itself in arithmetic: ONGC went ex-dividend and fell back under its breakout line on the same day its crude condition turned firm. Every published setup stays on the board until it completes (target hit) or fails (stop hit). Per-share prices on every line; the Rs 1,500 price cap screens what qualifies as a setup. Ledger: ' + a(LEDGER, 'Slow Burn swing research, 24 Oct') + '.'),
 chart('BOARD_TABLE'),
 chart(' ONGC 218.5 224.75 238.75 ONGC: coil floor to coil top, marker at Thursday\'s close '),
 p(' The resolved line stays in the ledger: HCLTech, filled 28 October at the worst fill of Rs 1,378.00, target Rs 1,466.80 taken out at 1,468.00 - gross +6.44%, net +6.14% after the 0.3% cost convention, in seven sessions; the stock closed Thursday at Rs 1,378.00, exactly its old worst-fill price, which changes nothing for a line that already paid. Of the open lines: ONGC (Rs 224.75) closed back below the 228 breakout line after six sessions above it - but Thursday was its ex-dividend date for the Rs 8 interim, so most of the Rs 10.25 fall is the payout leaving the price, and the crude condition has now firmed twice over, with Brent settling at $95.82 on the Saudi attacks; the coil floor at Rs 218.50 remains the invalidation that decides, not the argument. Kotak (Rs 488.70) has held above its Rs 437.55 trigger for thirteen sessions - a candidate, not a setup. Infosys (Rs 1,147.90) held flat below its Rs 1,175 trigger zone. JSW Steel (Rs 1,354.00) keeps failing the published R:R screen. Hindalco (Rs 964.20) remains a candidate without an entry zone. No new setup clears this morning - Thursday\'s leaders (Bajaj Finance at Rs 1,110.70, SBI at Rs 1,085) sit under the cap but carry no verified levels against longer history yet; an empty slot stays a result. Voltas stays rejected; Tech Mahindra stays shelved above the cap.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'How the board scores', 'blocks': [
 rows([
  row('1', 'No prior-day stop trade', 'A setup activates only if the zone fills before any prior-day trade at or below the stop.'),
  row('2', 'Same-day zone and stop', 'Unknowable from daily data: conservative scoring assumes the worst fill, then the stop.'),
  row('3', 'Resolutions stay on record', 'A setup leaves the board only when its target or stop resolves it; winners and losers both stay in the ledger.'),
 ]),
]})

G.append({'heading': "The editor's read", 'blocks': [
 p(' Three conditions, in the order they resolve. First, this morning\'s referendum: Mumbai opens having never priced either of Thursday\'s two shocks - the 6.09% print landed after the bell and Brent\'s $3 jump settled after that. Watch whether 25,000 holds, whether the foreign tide\'s seventeenth session becomes an eighteenth, and whether ONGC\'s sellers read the ex-date gap or the $95.82 barrel. Second, the succession: Kress holds two jobs and one deadline - the 17 November print, now the most scrutinised earnings call in the market - while Jones runs the first external-facing board Nvidia has ever had. Every day without a named successor is a day the key-person discount stays open; every customer or export decision taken in the interim is taken under a caretaker. Third, the two Decembers: the Fed\'s meeting is fully priced for at least a 25-point hike with tonight\'s producer prices the next input, and the RBI\'s poll moved to a second straight hike on Thursday\'s breach - both sides of the rupee\'s exchange rate tightening into the same month, with gold already broken and the ten-year at 5.10%. This desk does not predict the answers; it watches these conditions.'),
]})

G.append({'heading': 'Brewing', 'blocks': [
 p(' The succession file. Cause chain: reporting in October linking the founder to a suspected intelligence asset; Wachtell hired to investigate; a staff letter on Sunday; a board meeting; Thursday\'s one-sentence exit with no reason given - and a 4.34% close cushioned by the biggest retail bid on record. Watch next: the permanent-search shortlist, whether Kress keeps both jobs into the print, the 17 November call\'s first question, and whether customers or export licences treat the caretaker differently. Proven wrong if: a successor is named fast, the print lands clean, and the governance premium vanishes inside a fortnight.'),
 p(' The barrel\'s second wind. Cause chain: the Gulf war\'s eighth month; tankers burning at sea; a Saudi pipeline station, an Aramco building and an oil facility claimed hit in one day; Hormuz disruptions; the president posting that America needs no deal with Iran; Brent at $95.82, up $3.06 - with India\'s own print at 6.09% landing the same afternoon. Watch next: any Saudi or American military answer, the strait\'s daily ship counts, and India\'s pass-through - pump prices, the import bill, and ONGC\'s coil. Proven wrong if: the attacks stop, the strait normalises, and Brent hands the $3 back within days.'),
 p(' The two Decembers. Cause chain: America\'s hot CPI on Tuesday, Fed speakers splitting on speed, a December hike fully priced with a tail on fifty; India\'s twelve-month climb to 6.09%, food at 8.94%, the RBI poll moving to 5.75%; gold at a one-year low, the ten-year at 5.102%, the dollar at a nineteen-month high - and one rupee at 94.04 caught between the two central banks. Watch next: tonight\'s US producer prices, the RBI\'s December statement language, and whether the foreign tide keeps paying nine-month-high prices into a tightening cycle. Proven wrong if: either December passes with no move and the print that caused it all revised away.'),
]})

